[[File:Modding-First-Mod.png|thumb|right|The ShroudForge Modloader can show a mod's settings and status.]]

This beginner guide takes about 10–15 minutes. You need a text editor. You do not need prior programming experience.

A mod package has one required information file, a Lua file for the instruction in this example, and an optional settings file:

* <code>mod.json</code> describes the mod, its version, and the capabilities it requests.
* <code>src/mod.lua</code> contains the Lua code.
* <code>extended.mod.json</code> is optional. It can define player-facing settings.

== 1. Make a folder for the mod ==

Open your Enshrouded game folder, then open its <code>mods</code> folder. Make a new folder called <code>yourname.first-mod</code>.

<pre>
mods/yourname.first-mod/
├── mod.json
└── src/
    └── mod.lua
</pre>

== 2. Add mod.json ==

Create <code>mod.json</code> in the new folder. Change the ID, name, author, and description to match your mod.

<pre>
{
  "$schema": "https://bonsaibauer.github.io/shroudforge/schemas/manifest.schema.json",
  "id": "yourname.first-mod",
  "name": "My First Mod",
  "version": "1.0.0",
  "authors": ["Your name"],
  "description": "Writes a message.",
  "license": null,
  "icon": null,
  "capabilities": [],
  "dependencies": []
}
</pre>

The <code>id</code> should uniquely identify the package. The <code>name</code> is the display name. Leave <code>version</code> as <code>1.0.0</code> for this example. The empty <code>capabilities</code> and <code>dependencies</code> lists are enough for this example.

See [[mod.json]] for the fields and the canonical schema.

== 3. Add the Lua instruction ==

Inside the <code>src</code> folder, create <code>mod.lua</code> and add this line:

<pre>
shroudforge.log.info("My first mod loaded")
</pre>

This sends a message to the ShroudForge log.

== 4. Find the mod in the game ==

Start Enshrouded. Press <kbd>F9</kbd> to open the Mods window. Your mod should appear in the list. Press <kbd>F10</kbd> to open the messages and look for <code>My first mod loaded</code>.

If ShroudForge cannot find the mod, check that its folder is inside the game's <code>mods</code> folder. Downloaded mods can use different folder layouts, so follow their creator's instructions.

== Next steps ==

Add player-facing switches or sliders with [[extended.mod.json]]. For Lua features, start with [[Modding with Lua]] and [[Modding API guides]].

